{
  "id": "Compound::DB00771 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Clidinium, a cholinergic antagonist, is implicated in the relationship with Chrm3 through a series of mechanistic links. The evidence suggests that Clidinium, as part of the cholinergic antagonist class, binds to Chrm1, which is a G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor. This receptor activity is then associated with Chrm3, indicating a potential role in the signaling pathways involving cholinergic receptors. The lowest common ancestor of Clidinium and Aclidinium points to Carboxylic Ester, a class of compounds that may contribute to the overall mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways implies that Clidinium may modulate Chrm3 through interactions with G-protein coupled receptors, potentially influencing receptor activity and downstream signaling. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Clidinium and Chrm3, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
